xposed or concealed.\\n        * Half Flush: A hand consisting of only honor tiles and suited tiles of one suit. Example: [W1, W2, W3, W5, W5, W5, W6, W7, W8, F1, F1, F1, J3, J3]\\n        * Mixed Shifted Chows: A hand with three consecutive chows in different suits, each shifted up one number from the one before it. Example: [W3, W4, W5, B4, B5, B6, T5, T6, T7, W5, W5, W5, B9, B9]\\n        * All Types: A hand containing tiles of all three suits, wind tiles and dragon tiles. Example: [W1, W2, W3, B4, B4, B4, T7, T8, T9, F2, F2, F2, J1, J1]\\n        * Melded Hand: Every meld and pair in the hand must be completed by tiles from other players, which means that all melds must be exposed, and one tile of the pair is the 14th winning tile from other players.\\n        * Two Concealed Kongs: A hand with two concealed kongs.\\n        * Two Dragon Pungs: A hand with two pungs or kongs of dragon tiles.\\n    Patterns worth 4 points:\\n        * Outside Hand: A hand with terminals (number 1 or 9 of suited tiles) and honor tiles in every meld and the pair.\\n        * Fully Concealed Hand: A hand with no tiles from other players, which means that all melds must be concealed, and the winning tile is drawn by the winner.\\n        * Two Melded Kongs: A hand with two exposed kongs.\\n        * Last Tile: The winning tile is the last tile of the same tile that has not been revealed to all players, which means three of the same tile has been exposed in melds or discarded on the table.\\n    Patterns worth 2 points:\\n        * Dragon Pung: A hand with a pung or kong of dragon tiles. This pattern can be counted more than once if there are multiple melds meeting this requirement.\\n        * Prevalent Wind: A hand with a pung or kong of the prevalent wind of this game.\\n        * Seat Wind: A hand with a pung or kong of the seat wind of the current player.\\n        * Concealed Hand: A hand with no tiles from other players except the winning tile, which means that all melds must be concealed, while the winning tile is seized from other players.\\n        * All Chows: A hand with four chows and a pair of suited tiles.\\n        * Tile Hog: A hand with four identical tiles without forming a kong. This pattern can be counted more than once if there are multiple tiles meeting this requirement.\\n        * Mixed Double Pung: A hand with two pungs or kongs in different suits with the same number. This pattern can be counted more than once if there are multiple melds meeting this requirement.\\n        * Two Concealed Pungs: A hand with two concealed pungs or kongs.\\n        * Concealed Kong: A hand with a concealed kong.\\n        * All Simples: A hand with no terminal tiles (number 1 or 9 of suited tiles) and honor tiles.\\n    Patterns worth 1 points:\\n        * Pure Double Chow: A hand with two identical chows. This pattern can be counted more than once if there are multiple melds meeting this requirement.\\n        * Mixed Double Chow: A hand with two chows in different suits with the same number. This pattern can be counted more than once if there are multiple melds meeting this requirement.\\n        * Short Straight: A hand with two chows in the same suit with successive numbers, like 234 and 567. This pattern can be counted more than once if there are multiple melds meeting this requirement.\\n        * Two Terminal Chows: A hand with two chows in the same suit with numbers 1, 2, 3 and 7, 8, 9. This pattern can be counted more than once if there are multiple melds meeting this requirement.\\n        * Pung of Terminals or Honors: A hand with a pung or kong of terminal tiles (number 1 or 9 of suited tiles) or honor tiles. This pattern can be counted more than once if there are multiple melds meeting this requirement.\\n        * Melded Kong: A hand with one exposed kong.\\n        * One Voided Suit: A hand with exactly two of the three suits of tiles.\\n        * No Honors: A hand with no honor tiles.\\n        * Edge Wait: The winning tile must be a suited tile of number 3 to complete a chow of 1, 2, 3, or number 7 to complete a chow of 7, 8, 9. This pattern is not counted if there exists multiple tiles to complete a winning pattern.\\n        * Closed Wait: The winning tile must be a suited tile to complete a chow as the middle number. This pattern is not counted if there exists multiple tiles to complete a winning pattern.\\n        * Single Wait: The winning tile must be the tile to complete a pair. This pattern is not counted if there exists multiple tiles to complete a winning pattern.\\n        * Self Drawn: The winning tile must be drawn by the winner.\\n    Now is your turn, to your best ability, please briefly evaluate a few proimsing moves, provide reason for your final choice, and output one of the valid actions with specified format, such as Play B5.\\n        Your response format should be exactly:\\n        Evaluation: [Your Evaluation]\\n        Reason: [Your Reason]\\n        Answer: [Your Answer]\\n    \", \"user_prompt\": \"The following is the state information: The Prevailing Wind is: North Wind.Your Seat Wind is: West Wind.\\nUnshown tiles are: [W1: 1; W2: 2; W3: 2; W4: 2; W5: 3; W6: 3; W7: 0; W8: 3; W9: 0; T1: 2; T2: 0; T3: 3; T4: 2; T5: 3; T6: 4; T7: 4; T8: 3; T9: 1; B1: 2; B2: 3; B3: 3; B4: 2; B5: 1; B6: 1; B7: 3; B8: 1; B9: 1; F1: 3; F2: 1; F3: 0; F4: 0; J1: 1; J2: 4; J3: 1].\\nYour hand tiles are: [B1: 1; B3: 1; B4: 1; J2: 2].\\nYour melds are: [[F3, F3, F3],[W2, W3, W4],[B9, B9, B9]].\\nThe melds of the next player: [[B5, B6, B7],[B1, B2, B3]].\\nThe melds of the player sitting across: [[B8, B8, B8]].\\nThe melds of the previous player: [[T2, T2, T2]].\\nYour game history is: Play W6, Play B6, Play W1, Peng F3, Play B8, Play W9, Play J3, Chi W3, Play F4, Play W2, Play W7, Play B3, Play T1, Play W7, Play W3, Play W9, Play J1, Peng B9.\\nThe game history of the next player is: Play F2, Chi B6, Play F3, Play W1, Play B5, Play B5, Play T2, Play T4, Play B6, Chi B2, Play W9, Play W7, Play T8, Play T9, Play B4, Play B9.\\nThe game history of the player sitting across is: Play T3, Play F2, Play F2, Play F3, Peng B8, Play B6, Play T5, Play J1, Play T2, Play J3, Play F4, Play W4, Play T9, Play W9, Play F1.\\nThe game history of the previous player is: Play T9, Play W7, Play B1, Play W1, Play F4, Play W3, Peng T2, Play T1, Play T4, Play J1, Play W8, Play J3, Play B4, Play W5, Play F4.\\nThe followings are your valid actions:[Play B1; Play B4; Play B3; Play J2]\\u3002\", \"reasoning\": \"\", \"output\": \"Let's evaluate the state.  \\n\\n**Hand tiles:** B1, B3, B4, J2, J2  \\n**Melds:**  \\n1. [F3, F3, F3] (pung of west wind)  \\n2. [W2, W3, W4] (chow)  \\n3. [B9, B9, B9] (pung of B9)  \\n\\n**Remaining tiles in hand:**  \\nWe need 14 tiles for a winning hand; currently we have:  \\n- Meld 1: 3 tiles  \\n- Meld 2: 3 tiles  \\n- Meld 3: 3 tiles  \\n- Hand: 5 tiles  \\n\\nSo far we have 3 melds, so we only need **1 more meld + 1 pair** to win, or could go for **Seven Pairs** or **Thirteen Orphans** (unlikely here).  \\n\\nLooking at hand: B1, B3, B4, J2, J2.  \\n- J2 is already a pair (2 tiles), so if we get one more meld we win.  \\n- Possible melds from current hand:  \\n  - Chow possibility: B1, B3, B4 \\u2014 not a sequence (B2 missing for B1-B2-B3 or B2-B3-B4).  \\n  - Pung possibility: no triples in hand except J2 already a pair.  \\n\\nThus our hand needs 1 tile to complete a meld (or 1 tile to form another pair for Seven Pairs if we break J2 pair).  \\n\\n**Remaining tile counts (unshown tiles left):**  \\nWe see unshown tiles given, for relevant ones:  \\n\\n- B2: 3 left \\u2014 could make B1-B2-B3 chow or B2-B3-B4 chow.  \\n- B5: 1 left \\u2014 B3-B4-B5 chow possible if we draw B5 (only 1 left).  \\n- B3: 3 left \\u2014 could pung B3 if we draw two more B3 (unlikely).  \\n- B4: 2 left \\u2014 could pung if draw two B4.  \\n- J2: 4 left \\u2014 could pung J2 if draw one more J2.  \\n\\nAlso B1: 2 left \\u2014 could pung B1 if draw two more B1.  \\n\\nGiven we already have J2 pair and need only 1 meld:  \\nThe quickest win is:  \\n- Draw J2 \\u2014 we have J2 pung + J2 pair? No, wait: If we draw J2, we have 3 J2 + 1 J2 leftover? That\\u2019s 4 J2, which is Kong plus one leftover tile that becomes useless. That would break the pair. So better: Keep J2 pair, and get a meld from B-suit.  \\n\\nWe need to discard one tile now.  \\n**Potential discards:**  \\n\\n1. **Play B1** \\u2014 keeps B3-B4 for B2 or B5 chow, plus J2 pair. Leaves possible chows: B3-B4-B5 or B2-B3-B4.  \\n   - B2: 3 left in wall.  \\n   - B5: 1 left in wall.  \\n\\n2. **Play B3** \\u2014 leaves B1, B4, J2 pair. Possible chows: B1-B2-B3 needs B2 (3 left) and we discard B3, so no, that chow gone; B4-B5-B6 needs B5, B6. Worse shape.  \\n\\n3. **Play B4** \\u2014 leaves B1, B3, J2 pair. Possible chows: B1-B2-B3 (needs B2) or B2-B3-B4 gone.  \\n\\n4. **Play J2** \\u2014 then we have B1, B3, B4 all singles, need to form two pairs + meld? Actually if we break J2 pair, we need a new pair from somewhere else, which means our hand becomes far from ready. That\\u2019s bad.  \\n\\n**Best to keep J2 pair** because it\\u2019s already a pair, and we only need 1 meld of 3 tiles to win.  \\n\\nAmong B1, B3, B4: which is least useful?  \\n- B3 and B4 together can form chow with B2 or B5 (B2-B3-B4 or B3-B4-B5).  \\n- B1 only helps with B2 (B1-B2-B3) and needs B2.  \\n\\nB1\\u2019s chow requires B2 (3 left) and B3 (we have 1 B3). So B1 is less connected because B2 is needed and then we need B3 which we have only 1 of (and we would use it in the B1-B2-B3 chow).  \\n\\nIf we keep B3-B4, they work with either B2 or B5. Two different tiles to complete chow (B2: 3 left, B5: 1 left). That\\u2019s 4 total tiles left in wall that complete chow for B3-B4.  \\n\\nIf we keep B1-B3, they only work with B2 (3 left in wall). That\\u2019s fewer total tiles to win.  \\n\\nSo discard B1 keeps more winning draws.  \\n\\nAlso B1 is terminal (B1) \\u2014 if we get All Simples? But we already have B9 pung and F3 pung, so All Simples impossible. No benefit for terminal.  \\n\\n**Wait possibilities:** If we keep B3-B4: Edge wait? No. Closed wait? Yes if we wait for B2 or B5. Single wait? No, pair is J2.  \\n\\n**Evaluating discarding B1 vs B4:**  \\n- Keep B3-B4: wait for B2 or B5.  \\n- Keep B1-B3: wait for B2 only.  \\n\\nSo discard B1 is better.  \\n\\nAlso see opponent melds: next player has B1-B2-B3 chow already, so B1 is less dangerous? Not necessarily, but less chance they need B1 for chow.  \\n\\nGiven all, discard B1 maximizes remaining winning tile draws.  \\n\\n**Evaluation:**  \\nDiscard B1 is best to keep B3-B4 for flexible chow completion, J2 pair kept.  \\n\\n**Reason:**  \\nB3-B4 together can complete a chow with B2 (3 left) or B5 (1 left), 4 total tiles to win; B1-B3 only completes with B2 (3 left). Also B1 is a terminal and less useful for hand-building given our current melds and pair.  \\n\\n**Answer:** Play B1\"}"}
